/// Chapter 15 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/15-data.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page Learn15Data(path: "/learn/15-data", title: "Fetching data", description: "Describe an API once with retries, caching and typed errors; show a resource with match; ask with http in an action; update optimistically; read data files.", layout: DocsShell("Fetching data", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.build"), blurb: "Get data onto a page: describe a service once, show a request's states, ask on demand, and read files at build time.", source: "md-docs/learn/15-data.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "choose-how-to-get-data", label: "Choose how to get data"), Anchor(id: "show-a-request-with-resource", label: "Show a request with resource"), Anchor(id: "describe-a-service-with-api", label: "Describe a service with api"), Anchor(id: "ask-on-demand-with-http", label: "Ask on demand with http"), Anchor(id: "read-a-file-at-build-time-with-data", label: "Read a file at build time with data"), Anchor(id: "lists-of-data-a-table-a-tree-a-search", label: "Lists of data: a table, a tree, a search"), Anchor(id: "where-data-lands-in-the-pre-rendered-page", label: "Where data lands in the pre-rendered page"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting"), Anchor(id: "quiz", label: "Quiz"), Anchor(id: "common-mistakes", label: "Common mistakes")], learned: ["Show a read's three cases — `.loading`, `.failed(e)`, `.loaded(value)` — with `resource` and `match`", "Tell a refresh (`refreshing`, the value stays) from a first load (`.loading`)", "Describe a service once with `api`, typed, retried, cached and cancelled", "Ask on demand with `http` in an action, handle its result, and roll back an `optimistic` change", "Read a JSON file at build time with `data`, and render a page per item with `paths:`"], prevTo: "/learn/14-forms", prevLabel: t("ch.14"), nextTo: "/learn/16-testing", nextLabel: t("ch.16"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter is about getting data onto a WebFluent page and sending data back: from a request the page makes when it shows, from a request an action makes on demand, and from a file the build reads.\n\nYou will be able to:\n\n- show a read's `.loading`, `.failed(e)` and `.loaded(value)` cases with `resource` and `match`;\n- refresh data without the page going blank;\n- describe a service once with `api`, so every call is typed, retried, cached and cancelled for you;\n- read a service's surface from an OpenAPI file;\n- ask on demand from an action, and show a change before the server agrees;\n- read a JSON file at build time and render one page per item in it.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You should know `state` and `action` ([State and reactivity](/learn/07-state-and-reactivity)), `match` ([Control flow](/learn/09-control-flow)) and how to declare a `type` ([Types](/learn/12-types)).\n\nThis chapter does not cover:\n\n- a connection the page holds open — a socket, a stream, a channel or a peer — which is [Open a socket](/recipes/open-a-socket);\n- working without a network, which is [Make a site work offline](/recipes/work-offline);\n- values fixed for each build, which are [Environments and secrets](/recipes/environments-and-secrets).")

        Heading("Choose how to get data", id: "choose-how-to-get-data").h2

        Table(caption: "Choose how to get data") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You want…").header
                    Table.Cell("Use").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Data the page shows as soon as it opens")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`resource`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Show a request with resource](#show-a-request-with-resource)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("One place that describes a service and its endpoints")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`api`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Describe a service with api](#describe-a-service-with-api)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A request made when something happens: a save, a delete, a step in a flow")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`http.post(…)` in an action") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Ask on demand with http](#ask-on-demand-with-http)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Content that is known at build time")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`data`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Read a file at build time with data](#read-a-file-at-build-time-with-data)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Show a request with resource", id: "show-a-request-with-resource").h2

        Markdown("To fetch data while a page shows, declare a `resource` and show its states with `match`. A *resource* is a request the page makes, and one value that says where it is: `.loading`, `.failed(e)` or `.loaded(value)`.\n\nThis page lists recent deploys, with a spinner while they load and an alert if the request fails:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Deploy { id: String, status: String, startedAt: String }

page Deploys(path: "/deploys", title: "Deploys", description: "Recent deploys.") {
    resource deploys: [Deploy] = http.get("/api/deploys")

    Heading("Deploys").h1
    match deploys {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .failed(e) { Alert("Could not load: {e.message}").danger }
        .loaded(rows) {
            if rows.length == 0 { Text("No deploys yet.").muted }
            for d in rows by d.id {
                Row(justify: .between) {
                    Text(d.id).bold
                    Badge(d.status, tone: if d.status == "ok" { .success } else { .danger })
                    Text(ago(d.startedAt)).muted.sm
                }
            }
        }
    }
    Button("Refresh") { on click { deploys.reload() } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Callout("**Coming from React?** There, a fetch is a `useEffect` that sets `loading`, `error` and `data` states by hand, or a `useQuery`. A `resource` is the request and its state together: no effect, no flags to keep in step, and a `match` with one arm per case. See [Coming from React](/coming-from/react).", kind: .note)

        Markdown("How it works:\n\n- `resource name: T = http.get(url)` starts the request when the page renders.\n- The annotation (`: [Deploy]`) types the value. A read of `http.get` must have one (`N11`); a read of an endpoint takes the endpoint's.\n- A read is one value in one of three cases, and a `match` over it has an arm for each, or an `else`:\n\n  | Case | When | |---|---| | `.loading` | nothing to show yet | | `.failed(e)` | the first load failed; `e` is an `HttpError` — or the endpoint's error enum, for one declared `-> T ! E` | | `.loaded(value, refreshing, stale)` | something to show; `refreshing` while it is asked again; `stale` the failure of a refresh, else `null` |\n\n  An arm names as many of its case's places as it uses: `.loaded(rows)`. One with no `.failed` arm and no `else` is `T21`; one that leaves out another case is `T15`.\n- The pre-rendered page — the HTML the build writes for a static site — shows the `.loading` arm. The live page fetches and replaces it.\n- A failed response (one that is not `ok`) is `.status(code, problem)`, whose `e.message` is `HTTP 500`, or whatever the status was.")

        Heading("Read one fact of a resource anywhere", id: "read-one-fact-of-a-resource-anywhere").h3

        Markdown("Without a `match`, a resource has these, each worked out from its one value, so they cannot disagree. Each is live wherever you read it — a label, a splice, an attribute — with no `derived` in between:")

        Table(caption: "Read one fact of a resource anywhere") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Value").header
                    Table.Cell("What it is").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.value`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The value, while it is `.loaded`; else `null` (`T?`)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.isLoading` · `name.isLoaded` · `name.isFailed`") }
                    Table.Cell("Which case it is in")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.isRefreshing`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true` while a refresh runs ([Refresh without clearing the page](#refresh-without-clearing-the-page))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.error`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("What went wrong: the first load's failure, or the last refresh's (`E?`)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.pending`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true` while any request of it runs, the first load included") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.reload()`") }
                    Table.Cell("Makes the request again")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.invalidate()`") }
                    Table.Cell("Drops what the cache holds for it, and asks again")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.cancel()`") }
                    Table.Cell("Aborts the request that is running")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("So `Button(if plan.isLoaded \{ \"Request approval\" \} else \{ \"Deploy\" \})` changes its label when the plan arrives, and `if let .failed(e) = plan \{ Alert(e.message).danger \}` shows one case without a `match`.")

        Heading("Refresh without clearing the page", id: "refresh-without-clearing-the-page").h3

        Markdown("These ask for a resource again:\n\n- an argument, or a part of the address, a body or a header, that reads state and changes;\n- the `refresh:` option of `http.get(…)` or of an endpoint's call: `.onReconnect` (when the network returns — what a read does when it says nothing), `.onFocus` (when the reader comes back to the tab), `.every(30.seconds)`, `.never`, or a list of them;\n- `name.reload()` and `name.invalidate()`.\n\nAsking again for the request already shown is a *refresh*. The page stays as it is while a refresh runs: the read stays `.loaded`, with its value and `refreshing` true, and the answer is drawn in place.\n\nThis page polls a list of servers every 30 seconds, and shows a small spinner during each refresh:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Server { id: String, name: String, status: String }

page Servers(path: "/servers", title: "Servers", description: "Every server, kept current.") {
    state stage = "test"
    resource servers: [Server] = http.get("/api/servers?env={stage}", refresh: .every(30.seconds))

    Heading("Servers").h1
    Button("Production") { on click { stage = "prod" } }
    match servers {
        .loading { Skeleton(height: "20px") }
        .failed(e) { Alert(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list, refreshing, stale) {
            if stale != null { Alert("Could not refresh; this is what was there.").warning }
            if refreshing { Spinner.sm }
            for s in list by s.id { Text("{s.name}: {s.status}") }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Callout("**Coming from TanStack Query?** `isLoading` is `name.isLoading`, true only while there is nothing to show; `isFetching` is `name.pending`; and `name.isRefreshing` is a fetch while data is on screen. The three cases are its `status`, made one value. See [Coming from React](/coming-from/react).", kind: .note)

        Markdown("What a refresh keeps:\n\n- **The arm.** The `.loaded` arm's bindings are live. Text, attributes, `if`, `if let`, a keyed `for … by` and component props inside the arm follow the new value. The arm is drawn again only when the case changes.\n- **The rows.** A row whose key is still in the list keeps its nodes, its focus and its components' state. An input keeps what was typed.\n- **Unchanged data.** The parts of an answer equal to what was shown are kept as the same objects. An item is matched by its `id`, by its value, or by its place. An answer that changed nothing redraws nothing.\n\nWhat happens when a request fails, or changes:\n\n- A refresh that fails stays `.loaded`, with the value, and its failure is `stale` until the next answer, so the arm can say the value is out of date.\n- A first load that fails is `.failed(e)`, and its `reload()` is a first load again.\n- A request that changed is a first load again, shown as `.loading`. A request changes when an argument that reads state now names something else — another environment, another page — because the value on screen would be the wrong one. What the last request brought goes with it: `name.value` is `null` (and a paged resource's `name.items` empty) until the answer arrives, and stays so if it fails, so a `derived` value over it never reads the other request's answer. With `paginate:`, the next page is a refresh instead.\n- `placeholder: .previous` keeps the last value shown instead, `.loaded` and refreshing, until the new answer arrives — a search whose results stay while the next ones load.\n\nPressing **Production** above changes `stage`, so the list shows its `.loading` arm and then the production servers.")

        Heading("Send options with a request", id: "send-options-with-a-request").h3

        Markdown("The verb is the method — `http.get`, `http.post`, `http.put`, `http.patch`, `http.delete` — and options follow the URL, by name:\n\n- `headers:` — a map;\n- `body:` — what is sent, as JSON with the content type set.\n\nThe answer is read as JSON and is the type the resource declares.\n\nThis page searches with a `POST` and an authorization header:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Hit { id: String, title: String }

page Search(path: "/search", title: "Search", description: "Search the catalogue.") {
    state q = ""
    state token = "demo-token"
    resource hits: [Hit] = http.post("/api/search",
        headers: { Authorization: "Bearer {token}" },
        body: { query: q, limit: 20 })

    Heading("Search").h1
    Input(bind: q, label: "Search")
    match hits {
        .loading { Spinner.sm }
        .failed(e) { Text(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list) { Text("{list.length} hits") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Fetch again when state changes", id: "fetch-again-when-state-changes").h3

        Markdown("A URL that reads state is fetched again whenever that state changes. Use it for a search box that fetches as the reader types, or a detail page that follows its route parameter:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Post { slug: String, title: String, body: String }

page PostPage(path: "/posts/:slug", title: "Post", description: "One post.", slug: String) {
    resource post: Post = http.get("/api/posts/{slug}")
    match post {
        .loading { Skeleton }
        .failed(e) { Alert("Not found").warning }
        .loaded(p) {
            Heading(p.title).h1
            Markdown(p.body)
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A value spliced into the address is URL-encoded for you where it stands for a value.\n\nTo fetch less often than the reader types, derive the URL from a slower value. This page keeps what the reader types in `draft`, and searches only when they press Enter:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Hit { id: String, title: String }

page Lookup(path: "/lookup", title: "Lookup", description: "Search when you press Enter.") {
    state draft = ""
    state submitted = ""
    resource hits: [Hit] = http.get("/api/search?q={submitted}")

    Heading("Lookup").h1
    Input(bind: draft, label: "Search") { on key("Enter") { submitted = draft } }
    match hits {
        .loading { Spinner.sm }
        .failed(e) { Text(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list) { Text("{list.length} results for {submitted}") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Describe a service with api", id: "describe-a-service-with-api").h2

        Markdown("To call a service from many places, describe it once with `api`: its address, how it is reached and what endpoints it has. Every call site is then typed, cached and cancellable.\n\nThis service has four endpoints, and the page shows a list of users with a different alert for each kind of failure:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }
type NewUser { name: String }

/// Why adding someone can be refused.
enum CreateUserError {
    unprocessableContent(problem: Problem) on 422
    http(error: HttpError)
}

api Backend(base: "/api/v1") {
    credentials: .sameOrigin      // the session cookie goes with every call
    timeout: 10.seconds
    retry: .backoff(times: 3, on: [.network, .timeout, .status5xx])

    get    users(page: Number = 1, q: String?) at "users"  -> [User]!
    get    user(id: String) at "users/{id}"      -> User!
    post   createUser(body: NewUser) at "createUser"            -> User ! CreateUserError
    delete removeUser(id: String) at "users/{id}" -> !
}

page Users(path: "/users", title: "Users", description: "Everyone.") {
    state page: Number = 1
    resource rows = Backend.users(page: page, cache: .swr(60.seconds), refresh: .onFocus)

    Heading("Users").h1
    match rows {
        .loading { Skeleton(height: "20px") }
        .failed(e) {
            match e {
                .offline { Alert("You are offline.").warning }
                .timeout { Alert("The server took too long.").warning }
                else     { Alert(e.message).danger }
            }
            Button("Try again") { on click { rows.reload() } }
        }
        .loaded(list) { for u in list by u.id { Text(u.name) } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- A parameter the path names goes in the path. Every other parameter goes in the query string.\n- `->` says what a success returns and how a call fails: `-> [User]!` is a list of users or an `HttpError`, `-> User ! CreateUserError` a user or a case of that error enum, `-> !` nothing but may fail ([Say how a call fails](#say-how-a-call-fails)). A `resource` over the endpoint holds what a success returns, so `u.nam` is `T05` before the page ever runs.\n- An endpoint the service does not have is `T06`, and an argument it does not take is `T10`.")

        Heading("Pass every request through middleware", id: "pass-every-request-through-middleware").h3

        Markdown("What every call of a service carries — a token, the reader's language, a request ID — and what it does when the server says a token expired, is *middleware*: steps each request passes through, in the order written in the service's `middleware [ … ]`. Each step is handed the request and the rest of the chain, `next`, and returns what `next` returns.\n\nThis service refreshes the token on a `401`, sends it as a bearer token, sends the reader's language, and tags each request with an ID:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }
type Token { token: String }

store Session {
    state accessToken = ""
    state language = "en"
    action refresh() -> ! {
        let fresh: Token = try await http.post("/api/token")
        accessToken = fresh.token
    }
}

api Backend(base: env.PUBLIC_API) {     // its default in the config's `env`
    middleware [refreshOn401(Session.refresh), bearer(Session.accessToken), header("Accept-Language", Session.language), requestId]

    get me at "me" -> User!
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The runtime's steps:")

        Table(caption: "Pass every request through middleware") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Step").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`bearer(token)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Authorization: Bearer …`, read when the request reaches it; nothing when the token is empty") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`header(name, value)`") }
                    Table.Cell("One header, read when the request reaches it")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`refreshOn401(action)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("On a `401`, runs the action and sends the request again through the steps after it. Requests that hear `401` at the same time share one run of the action, since a running action is joined. Put it before `bearer`, so the request sent again carries the new token") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`requestId`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A fresh `X-Request-Id` for each request") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`log`") }
                    Table.Cell("Each request, and how it ended, in the console")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A step of your own is a `middleware` declaration. Its two names are the request — a `Request`: `url`, `method`, `headers` (a `[String: String]`) and `body` — and the rest of the chain:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Me { name: String }

/// Every request, timed.
middleware timed(req, next) {
    let started = Date.now()
    let answer = await next(req)
    log("{req.method} {req.url} took {Date.now() - started} ms")
    return answer
}

/// What every request sends about the client.
middleware client(req, next) {
    return await next(Request(url: req.url, method: req.method, headers: req.headers.with("X-Client", "web"), body: req.body))
}

api Accounts(base: "/accounts") {
    middleware [client, timed]
    get me at "me" -> Me!
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("What `next` returns is a result: `.ok(json)` with the answer before it is decoded, or `.err(e)` with an `HttpError`. A step may look at it, call `next` again, or answer without the network. A step that throws fails the call with `.transport`.")

        Heading("What every call does for you", id: "what-every-call-does-for-you").h3

        Table(caption: "What every call does for you") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Feature").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Abort")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A call is aborted when its scope leaves, when an argument that reads state changes, on `.cancel()`, and on timeout") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Errors")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("an `HttpError`: `.offline`, `.timeout(after)`, `.cancelled`, `.transport(message)`, `.decode(at, message)`, `.status(code, problem)` — each with `.message`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Decoding")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("What a call or a `resource` declares it returns is held to what arrives, in every build: a server that breaks its contract fails as `.decode(at, message)`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Retry")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Exponential backoff with jitter, per error kind, honouring `Retry-After` — for a request that is safe to send twice, a `POST` or `PATCH` only when its endpoint is `idempotent`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Cache")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("What the server's `Cache-Control` allows (RFC 9111): fresh for its `max-age`, stale within `stale-while-revalidate`, revalidated with `ETag`, never under `no-store`, kept apart by `Vary` and the credentials; nothing without caching headers. `cache: .maxAge(60.seconds)`, `.swr(…)`, `.none`, `.forever` override it. It holds the 256 answers read most recently") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Dedupe")
                    Table.Cell("Two readers of one address make one request, cached or not")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Refetch")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`refresh: .onFocus`, `.onReconnect`, `.every(30.seconds)`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Pagination")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`paginate: .page` gives `.items`, `.loadMore()` and `.hasMore`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Progress")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `File` parameter makes `Backend.avatar.progress` a number from 0 to 1") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Beside each endpoint you also get `Backend.users.invalidate()`, `.prefetch(args)`, `.url(args)` and `.key(args)`. `invalidate()` drops what the cache holds, and every resource over the endpoint on the page asks again, so a list redraws after the write that changed it.")

        Heading("Groups, and what a write makes stale", id: "groups-and-what-a-write-makes-stale").h3

        Markdown("A service's endpoints can be split into groups by what they are about. A group is reached through its service and sends what its service sends: its `base`, `timeout`, `retry` and `middleware`. A group that sets its own is `N08`.\n\nA write can say which reads it changes with `invalidates`. Each name is a `get` of the same group, and each argument is one of the write's own parameters (`N05`). When the write succeeds, every resource on the page over one of those reads asks again, and the answer is drawn in place:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String }
type NewTodo { title: String }
type Rename { title: String }

api Backend(base: "/api") { }

api Backend.todos {
    get   list() at "todos" -> [Todo]!
    get   byId(id: String) at "todos/{id}" -> Todo!
    post  create(body: NewTodo) at "todos" -> Todo!
    invalidates [list]
    patch rename(id: String, body: Rename) at "todos/{id}" -> Todo!
    idempotent
        invalidates [list, byId(id)]
}

store Drafts {
    action add(title: String) {
        let undo = Backend.todos.list.update(list => list.concat([Todo(id: "new", title: title)]))
        if let .err(_) = await Backend.todos.create(body: NewTodo(title: title)) { undo() }
    }
}

page Todos(path: "/todos", title: "Todos", description: "The list.") {
    resource todos = Backend.todos.list()
    Heading("Todos").h1
    Button("Add bread") {
        on click { Backend.todos.create(body: NewTodo(title: "Bread")) }
    }
    match todos {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .failed(e) { Alert(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list) { for todo in list by todo.id { Text(todo.title) } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("To show a change before the server agrees, call `.update(change)` on a read. It changes what every resource over that read shows, and returns a function that takes the change back. `Drafts.add` in the example above shows the new todo at once and takes it back if the server refuses it.\n\nA `resource` must be over a read; a resource over a write is `N02`. A call awaited on its own, with nothing reading its result, is `N04`, because a failure would go unheard.")

        Heading("Read the service from an OpenAPI file", id: "read-the-service-from-an-openapi-file").h3

        Markdown("To take every endpoint from a specification instead of writing them, name the file with `from`:")

        CodeBlock(#"api Backend from "openapi.json" (base: env.PUBLIC_API)"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The build reads every endpoint, parameter, response type and error shape from the file, and each named schema becomes something the program can name. The day the server changes its contract, the build says so. A missing `openapi.json` is `E111`.\n\nHow each schema becomes a type:")

        Table(caption: "Read the service from an OpenAPI file") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The schema").header
                    Table.Cell("What the program gets").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An object: `\{ \"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `type` with those fields; a field the schema does not `require` is `T?`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An `allOf`: `[\{ \"$ref\": \"…/EnvSummary\" \}, \{ \"properties\": … \}]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`type ServerDetail = EnvSummary \{ … \}`: it extends the first record it names, and every other part adds fields of its own") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A string with cases: `\{ \"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"prod\", \"test\"] \}`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`enum Env \{ prod, test \}`, whose cases are the strings the server sends") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A string with a case no name can spell: `\"operate.flags\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`String`, wherever the schema is named") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Anything else: a string, a number, a list, another schema's `$ref`, a `oneOf` of one type and `null`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("That type, wherever the schema is named; a `oneOf` of several types is `Any`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A value that may be null becomes its type made optional, however the document says so:\n\n- OpenAPI 3.1's `\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]` is `String?`;\n- a `oneOf` of `\{ \"type\": \"null\" \}` and a `$ref` is that type's `?`;\n- 3.0's `\"nullable\": true` is the same, beside a `type`, an `allOf` or a `$ref`;\n- a record or an enum whose own schema allows null is `T?` wherever it is named.\n\nA string's `format` gives the language's type for what the server sends:")

        Table(caption: "Read the service from an OpenAPI file") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell.header { Markdown("`format`") }
                    Table.Cell("In a response, and in every record only responses reach").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`date-time`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`DateTime`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`date`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Date`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`uuid`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Uuid`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`email`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Email`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`uri`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Url`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`time`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`String`, everywhere: JSON Schema's carries an offset (`09:30:00Z`) that `Time` does not read") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("What the program sends — a parameter, a request body, and every record one of them reaches, except for the fields marked `readOnly` — keeps `String`. So a value the program holds as a `String`, such as a route parameter or what an input holds, can still be sent.\n\nAlso:\n\n- A parameter or a request body may be a `$ref` into `components`, as a shared `limit` often is.\n- A body is required when the document says so.\n- A `type` or an `enum` the program declares itself wins over the specification's of the same name.")

        Heading("Service settings", id: "service-settings").h3

        Markdown("These go in the service's parentheses or at the top of its block, before the endpoints:")

        Table(caption: "Service settings") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Setting").header
                    Table.Cell("Values").header
                    Table.Cell("Default").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`base:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The address every path is relative to: `\"/api/v1\"`, `env.PUBLIC_API`") }
                    Table.Cell("—")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`timeout:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A duration after which a call gives up with `.timeout`: `10.seconds`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`30.seconds`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`retry:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A count (`3`), `.never`, or `.backoff(times: 3, on: [.network, .timeout, .status5xx], delay: 300, max: 30000, jitter: true)`") }
                    Table.Cell("no retries")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`cache:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Overrides what the server says: `.maxAge(5.minutes)`, `.swr(60.seconds)` (serve what is held, revalidate behind it), `.forever`, `.none`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("what the server's `Cache-Control` says") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`credentials:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.omit`, `.sameOrigin`, `.include` — whether cookies go with a call") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the browser's (`.sameOrigin`)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`mode:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.cors`, `.noCors`, `.sameOrigin`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.cors`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`redirect:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.follow`, `.error`, `.manual`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.follow`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`referrer:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A referrer policy: `.noReferrer`, `.origin`, …") }
                    Table.Cell("the page's")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`middleware [ … ]`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The steps every request passes through, in order ([Pass every request through middleware](#pass-every-request-through-middleware))") }
                    Table.Cell("none")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("How `retry` works:\n\n- Its `on:` takes `.network`, `.timeout`, `.status5xx`, `.status4xx`, and single codes in the form `.status429` or `.status503`.\n- Without `on:`, it retries network failures, timeouts, 5xx responses and 429.\n- The wait doubles from `delay` (in milliseconds) up to `max`, with jitter. A `Retry-After` from the server wins.\n- An aborted call is never retried.\n- Only a request that is safe to send twice is retried: `GET`, `HEAD`, `PUT` and `DELETE`. A `POST` or `PATCH` that timed out may have reached the server, and a second one could be a second order, so it fails instead — unless its endpoint is marked `idempotent`: `post order(body: Order) at \"order\" -> Order! idempotent`. That sends one `Idempotency-Key` with every try of a call, so a server that keeps the key does the order once, and the call is retried, and kept to replay offline, like a `PUT`. A `retry:` on a call of any other write is `N09`.")

        Heading("Declare endpoints", id: "declare-endpoints").h3

        Markdown("Each line of a service, after its settings, is an endpoint:")

        CodeBlock(#"get users(page: Number = 1, q: String?) at "users" -> [User]!
get user(id: String) at "users/{id}" -> User ! LookupError
post createUser(body: NewUser) at "createUser" -> User ! CreateUserError
put rename(id: String, body: Rename) at "users/{id}" -> User!
patch settings(body: Settings) at "settings" -> Settings!
delete removeUser(id: String) at "users/{id}" -> !
get report(id: String) at "reports/{id}" -> Json! as: .blob"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("- **The verb** is `get`, `post`, `put`, `patch`, `delete`, `head` or `options`.\n- **The path.** `at` is required: where the server answers the endpoint, below the service's `base`.\n- **Path parameters.** A parameter the path names (`\{id\}`) goes in the path, and every `\{name\}` must be one of the endpoint's parameters (`N01`). One that is empty (`null` or `\"\"`) is not sent at all, since `users/` would be the whole collection, not the user; nor is one that is `.` or `..`, which the browser reads as this folder or the one above. The call ends as `.cancelled`, and a resource over it stays `.loading` until the value arrives.\n- **The body.** A parameter called `body` is the request body; a map is sent as JSON. A `File` parameter makes the call a multipart upload.\n- **Query parameters.** Every other parameter goes in the query string, and one that is `null` is left out.\n- **Headers and cookies.** `token: String in header \"X-Token\"` sends a parameter as a header, and `theme: String? in cookie` as a cookie.\n- **The first parameter** may be passed without its name: `Backend.user(\"7\")`.\n- **What comes back.** `-> T!` is a success's `T`, or an `HttpError`; `-> T ! E` names the failures in an error enum `E`, each status a case with its typed body; `-> !` returns nothing, as `204 No Content` does, and may fail. Under `wf serve` the body that arrives is held to `T`, and a declared status's body to its case's type. What crosses is a type the program names: `Map` and `Any` there are `N11`.\n- **Other responses.** `as:` says how to read a response that is not JSON: `.text`, `.blob`, `.arrayBuffer` or `.none`. `errorAs:` does the same for a failure's body. `cache:` sets a cache policy for that endpoint alone.\n\nA call passes the endpoint's parameters by name, and one with no default that may not be null must be given. Called in an action, it is a promise of a result: `await` it, then `match` it or pass its failure up with `try` ([Say how a call fails](#say-how-a-call-fails)). It may add `cache:`, `headers:`, `timeout:` and `retry:`, and in a `resource` also `on:` (`.focus`, `.reconnect`, `.interval(30.seconds)`) and `paginate:`.")

        Heading("Say how a call fails", id: "say-how-a-call-fails").h3

        Markdown("An endpoint that says how it fails returns a *result* rather than throwing: `-> T ! E` is a `T`, or an error of the *error enum* `E`, and `-> T!` is a `T` or an `HttpError`. An error enum names each status the service answers with as a case of its own, carrying the body that status sends, and keeps one case, `http(error: HttpError)`, for everything else:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }
type NewUser { name: String }
type FieldError { field: String, message: String }
type ValidationProblem = Problem { errors: [FieldError] }

/// Why adding someone can be refused.
enum CreateUserError {
    unprocessableContent(problem: ValidationProblem) on 422
    conflict(problem: Problem) on 409
    http(error: HttpError)
}

api Backend(base: "/api") {
    post createUser(body: NewUser) at "users" -> User ! CreateUserError
    get ping() at "ping" -> !
}

store People {
    state said = ""

    /// 1. Every case handled where the call is made.
    action submit(name: String) {
        match await Backend.createUser(body: NewUser(name: name)) {
            .ok(u) { said = "Added {u.name}" }
            .err(.unprocessableContent(p)) { said = p.errors.map(e => e.message).join(", ") }
            .err(.conflict(_)) { said = "Already there" }
            .err(.http(.status(503, _))) { said = "The service is busy" }
            .err(.http(e)) { said = e.message }
        }
    }

    /// 2. The failure passed up, as the action's own.
    action invite(name: String) -> User ! CreateUserError {
        let user = try await Backend.createUser(body: NewUser(name: name))
        return user
    }
}

page Team(path: "/people", title: "People", description: "Adding someone, and why it can fail.") {
    use People
    state name = ""
    Heading("People").h1
    Input(bind: name, label: "Name")
    Button("Add") { on click { People.submit(name) } }
    Button("Invite") { on click { People.invite(name) } }
    Text(People.said)
    // 3. The last outcome of an action, read where it is shown.
    if let .err(.unprocessableContent(p)) = People.invite.result {
        Alert(p.title ?? "Check the form").danger
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **An error enum.** Each case but `http` names its status, `on 422`, and carries one value, the body of that status. An enum that names a status is held to that shape (`N12`).\n- **`HttpError`** is what goes wrong between the page and the server: `.offline`, `.timeout(after)`, `.cancelled`, `.transport(message)`, `.decode(at, message)` and `.status(code, problem)`. Every one has `.message`, for showing.\n- **`Problem`** is a server's account of a failure as RFC 9457 has it — `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`, `extensions` — and a project extends it like any record. A status the enum does not name carries it as its `problem` when the server sends `application/problem+json`.\n- **A result** is `.ok(value)` (`.ok` when there is none) or `.err(error)`. A `match` on one gives every case an arm, or an `else`; an arm may look inside a case, `.err(.http(.status(503, _)))`, where `_` takes anything and a literal takes that value. `match` runs in an action or a handler too, and `if let .err(e) = outcome \{ … \}` takes one case.\n- **`try`** gives a result's value, or returns its error from the action as the action's own, which must say what it fails with (`N03`). The same error passes up as it is, an `HttpError` becomes an error enum's `.http`, and another error enum passes up through its `http` case once its other cases are handled. In a handler, `try` reports a failure instead.\n- **An action that may fail** returns its value, which is `.ok` of it; `return .err(e)` hands back a failure of its own. Its `.result` is its last outcome, or `null` before it has run.\n- **A `resource`** over such an endpoint holds its value, as it always has.")

        Heading("Load results a page at a time", id: "load-results-a-page-at-a-time").h3

        Markdown("To show a long list in pages, call the endpoint with `paginate: .page`. This page shows items and a **Load more** button while there are more:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Item { id: String, name: String }

api Catalog(base: "/api") {
    get items(page: Number = 1) at "items" -> [Item]!
}

page Items(path: "/items", title: "Items", description: "Everything, a page at a time.") {
    state page = 1
    resource list = Catalog.items(page: page, paginate: .page)

    Heading("Items").h1
    for item in list.items by item.id { Text(item.name) }
    if list.hasMore {
        Button("Load more") { on click { list.loadMore() } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `.items` gathers every page loaded so far. It is a list of what a page holds — the answer, when the endpoint returns a list, or its `items` field — so `item.name` is checked against `Item`.\n- `.loadMore()` moves `page` on by one and fetches the next page, as a refresh.\n- `.hasMore` turns false when a page comes back empty.")

        Heading("Call an API on another origin", id: "call-an-api-on-another-origin").h3

        Markdown("A page on `https://example.com` that calls `https://api.example.com` makes a *cross-origin request*. The API must answer with CORS headers — `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` if cookies go with the call — or the browser refuses the response.\n\nThe same applies while you develop. `wf serve` answers on `localhost:3000` and has no proxy, so an API on `localhost:8080` must allow that origin.\n\nThe alternative is to serve the API and the site from one origin behind a reverse proxy, where `base: \"/api\"` needs no CORS at all ([Deploying](/recipes/api-on-the-same-origin)).\n\nIf the page's build ships a content security policy, list the API's origin in `meta.connect` too ([Security](/about/security)).")

        Heading("Show a change before the server agrees", id: "show-a-change-before-the-server-agrees").h3

        Markdown("To make an edit appear at once instead of after the round trip, call `optimistic(holder, change)` before the request. If anything later in the action throws, the change is taken back: the action rolls itself back.")

        CodeBlock(#"action rename(id: String, name: String) {
    optimistic(Todos.items, items => items.map(i => if i.id == id { { ...i, title: name } } else { i }))
    await Backend.updateUser(id: id, body: { name: name })
    Backend.users.invalidate()
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The new title shows at once. If `updateUser` fails, the old title comes back.")

        Heading("Ask on demand with http", id: "ask-on-demand-with-http").h2

        Markdown("For a request made when something happens — a save, a delete, a step in a flow — call the built-in service `http` in an action: `http.get(url)`, `http.post(url, body: …)`, `http.put`, `http.patch`, `http.delete`. Its answer is a *result*, `.ok(value)` or `.err(e)` with an `HttpError`, so a failure is a value you handle rather than an exception you catch: pass it up with `try`, or `match` it where the call is made.\n\nThis page loads a list of todos, adds one, and toggles one, each with a request:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String, done: Bool = false }
type NewTodo { title: String }
type Change { done: Bool }

page Todos(path: "/todos", title: "Todos", description: "Todos saved to a server.") {
    state todos: [Todo] = []
    state draft = ""
    state error = ""

    action load() -> ! {
        todos = try await http.get("/api/todos")
    }

    action add() {
        if draft.trim() == "" { return }
        let saved: Todo! = await http.post("/api/todos", body: NewTodo(title: draft))
        match saved {
            .ok(created) {
                todos.push(created)
                draft = ""
            }
            .err(e) { error = "Could not save: {e.message}" }
        }
    }

    action toggle(id: String) -> ! {
        let found = todos.find(x => x.id == id)
        if let todo = found {
            let updated: Todo = try await http.patch("/api/todos/{id}", body: Change(done: !todo.done))
            todos = todos.map(x => if x.id == id { updated } else { x })
        }
    }

    load()
    Heading("Todos").h1
    Input(bind: draft, label: "New todo") { on key("Enter") { add() } }
    Button("Add", disabled: add.pending).primary { on click { add() } }
    if error != "" { Alert(error).danger }
    for todo in todos by todo.id {
        Checkbox(checked: todo.done, label: todo.title) { on change { toggle(todo.id) } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **What comes back is named where it goes**: `todos = try await http.get(…)` is a `[Todo]` because `todos` is, `let updated: Todo` names it, and `let saved: Todo!` names a whole result. A `get` whose answer goes nowhere that says what it is is `N11`; a write whose answer nothing reads needs no type.\n- **`try`** in `load` and `toggle` gives the answer, or ends the action with its failure as the action's own: each says what it fails with, `-> !` — nothing, or an `HttpError`. `add` instead matches the result and shows the failure.\n- `body:` is what is sent, as JSON; `headers:`, `timeout:`, `retry:` and `cache:` go beside it, and `as: .text` reads the answer as text.\n- `load()` at the top of the page is set-up code: it runs once, when the page renders.\n- `todos.push(created)` changes the state in place. The compiler turns it into a new list, so everything that reads `todos` repaints.\n- `name.pending` is `true` while the action runs, so **Add** is disabled during a save.\n- `http` goes through the same engine as an `api` call. `fetch` is the browser's own, `window.fetch`, with the raw response.")

        Heading("Read a file at build time with data", id: "read-a-file-at-build-time-with-data").h2

        Markdown("To use content that is known before the site is built — posts, a price list, a team page — read it with `data`. `data name = \"file.json\"` reads a JSON file from the project directory or `src/` at build time, and makes its value a constant.\n\nA `data` constant:\n\n- is in scope in every page and component;\n- costs nothing at run time, because its value is in the bundle;\n- is written into the pre-rendered page.\n\nThis blog lists every post in `posts.json`, newest first:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Post { slug: String, title: String, date: String, body: String }

data posts: [Post] = "posts.json"

page Blog(path: "/", title: "Blog", description: "Every post.") {
    derived sorted = posts.sortBy(p => p.date).reverse()

    Heading("Blog").h1
    for p in sorted by p.slug {
        Card {
            Link(p.title, to: "/posts/{p.slug}")
            Text(format(p.date, .date)).muted.sm
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        CodeBlock(##"[
  { "slug": "hello", "title": "Hello", "date": "2026-01-10", "body": "# Hi\nFirst post." },
  { "slug": "second", "title": "Second", "date": "2026-02-02", "body": "More." }
]"##, title: "json")

        Markdown("Annotate it (`data posts: [Post]`) and the checker knows every field — and the build holds the file to the type when it reads it. A record that is not a `Post` stops the build with `T33`, naming the file and the place: `` `posts.json` is not what `data posts` says it is: $[3].date is \"March 5\", not a Date ``.")

        Heading("Render a page for each item", id: "render-a-page-for-each-item").h3

        Markdown("To give each post its own HTML file in a static build, list the values of the route parameter in `paths:`. The build renders the page once per value, each with its own title and description, and lists each in the sitemap:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Post { slug: String, title: String, date: String, body: String }

data posts: [Post] = "posts.json"

page PostPage(path: "/posts/:slug", slug: String, title: "Post", description: "A post.", paths: posts.map(p => p.slug)) {
    derived post = posts.find(p => p.slug == slug)
    head { meta(property: "og:title", content: post?.title ?? "Post") }
    if let p = post {
        Heading(p.title).h1
        Markdown(p.body)
    } else {
        Alert("No such post").warning
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The build writes `posts/hello/index.html` and `posts/second/index.html`.\n\n- `paths:` must be known at build time: a `data` file, a `const` or a literal.\n- For a route with several parameters, each value is a map naming them: `paths: pairs.map(x => \{ year: x.y, slug: x.s \})`.\n- A route not in `paths:` still works in a single-page app. Only the static build needs the list.")

        Heading("Lists of data: a table, a tree, a search", id: "lists-of-data-a-table-a-tree-a-search").h2

        Markdown("Three built-ins draw rows from a list you hand them, each row with a block you write for it:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Deploy { id: String, service: String, status: String }
type Node { path: String, name: String, children: [Node] = [] }

page Ops(path: "/", title: "Ops", description: "Deploys, files and a search.") {
    state deploys: [Deploy] = [Deploy(id: "1", service: "web", status: "live")]
    state chosen: [String] = []
    state files: [Node] = [Node(path: "src", name: "src", children: [Node(path: "src/App.wf", name: "App.wf")])]
    state file = ""
    state who = ""
    Heading("Ops").h1
    DataTable(rows: deploys, key: d => d.id, bind: chosen, label: "Deploys").multiple {
        empty { Text("No deploys yet") }
        DataTable.Column("Service", value: d => d.service).sortable
        DataTable.Column("Status") { cell(d) { Badge(d.status) } }
    }
    Tree(items: files, children: f => f.children, key: f => f.path, bind: file, label: "Files") {
        row(f) { Text(f.name) }
    }
    Combobox(bind: who, options: ["Ada", "Alan", "Grace"], text: p => p, label: "Owner")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`DataTable`** declares its columns once. A `.sortable` column's header sorts the rows and says which way (`aria-sort`); `cell(d) \{ … \}` draws a cell; `empty \{ … \}` shows with no rows; `bind:` chooses rows by `key:`.\n- **`Tree`** walks `children:` from `items:`. The arrow keys open, close and move through it as a file tree does; `bind:` holds the chosen item's key.\n- **`Combobox`** offers the `options:` that match what is typed, or asks `search: q => …` once the typing rests; `option(o) \{ … \}` draws an offer.\n\nNamed blocks (`empty`, `row`, `cell`, `option`) come before an element's children. Each of these is drawn once the page runs; the static paint holds its place.")

        Heading("Where data lands in the pre-rendered page", id: "where-data-lands-in-the-pre-rendered-page").h2

        Markdown("A static build writes each page's HTML before any script runs. What each source of data puts in that HTML:")

        Table(caption: "Where data lands in the pre-rendered page") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Source").header
                    Table.Cell("In the pre-rendered HTML").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`data`, `const`, `env`") }
                    Table.Cell("Fully painted")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`state` initial values, and `derived` values over them") }
                    Table.Cell("Painted")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`resource`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The `.loading` arm; the live page fetches and replaces it") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`http` in an action") }
                    Table.Cell("Nothing until the action runs")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("Why, and what to do").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The browser console says a response was blocked by CORS")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The API is on another origin and does not allow yours. Add CORS headers to the API, or serve both from one origin ([Call an API on another origin](#call-an-api-on-another-origin)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A request gets a `404` under `wf serve`, with a line in the terminal") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`wf serve` has no file or page for that address, and no proxy. Point `base:` at the real API.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `.parse` error naming a place, such as `$[1].name is missing`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Under `wf serve`, a response is checked against its declared type. The server sent another shape: fix the server or the `type`.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A resource stays `.loading`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A path parameter is `null` or `\"\"`, so the call is not sent. It goes out when the value arrives.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The page goes back to `.loading` when state changes") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The address now names something else, so it is a first load. A refresh of the same address keeps the value; `placeholder: .previous` keeps it for a new address too.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T05` on a field of the data") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The declared type has no such field. Fix the name, or add the field to the `type`.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`T06` or `T10` on an endpoint call") }
                    Table.Cell("The service has no such endpoint, or the endpoint takes no such argument.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A post's page is missing from a static build")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Its value is not in `paths:`.") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Quiz", id: "quiz").h2

        Quiz("A resource's `match` has a `.loading` arm and a `.loaded` arm, and no other. What happens?", id: "Fetching data#10", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "The runtime draws an error message for you when the request fails", right: false, why: "The runtime draws only the arms you write."),
            QuizChoice(text: "The build stops with T21", right: true, why: "A failed first load is `.failed(e)`, which no arm takes, so the place would stay empty. Add `.failed(e) \{ … \}` or an `else`."),
            QuizChoice(text: "The `.loading` arm shows until a retry succeeds", right: false, why: "A first load that fails is `.failed(e)`, not `.loading`.")
        ])

        Quiz("A resource is making its very first request. Which is true?", id: "Fetching data#10", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "`pending` is true and `isRefreshing` is false", right: true, why: "`pending` covers every request of it, the first load included; `isRefreshing` only a request made again while a value is shown."),
            QuizChoice(text: "`isRefreshing` is true and `pending` is false", right: false, why: "It is the other way round: there is no value yet to refresh."),
            QuizChoice(text: "Both are true", right: false, why: "`isRefreshing` is true only while the read is `.loaded`, and on a first load it is `.loading`.")
        ])

        Quiz("A resource with `refresh: .every(30.seconds)` asks again while its list is on screen. What does its `match` show while that refresh runs?", id: "Fetching data#10", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "The `.loading` arm, then the new list", right: false, why: "Only a first load, or a request whose address changed, shows `.loading`."),
            QuizChoice(text: "An empty list until the answer arrives", right: false, why: "The value stays while a refresh runs; `name.value` is never cleared by one."),
            QuizChoice(text: "The `.loaded` arm with the old list, then the answer drawn in place", right: true, why: "A refresh stays `.loaded`, with `refreshing` true; rows whose key is still there keep their nodes.")
        ])

        Quiz("The address is `http.get(\"/api/servers?env=\{stage\}\")`, and a button sets `stage` from `\"test\"` to `\"prod\"`. What shows next?", id: "Fetching data#10", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "The `.loading` arm, then the production servers", right: true, why: "The request now names something else, so it is a first load; the test servers would be the wrong data."),
            QuizChoice(text: "The test servers, with `refreshing` true, until the production servers arrive", right: false, why: "That is a refresh, which asks again for the same request; this one changed. `placeholder: .previous` would ask for it."),
            QuizChoice(text: "Nothing changes until the reader reloads the page", right: false, why: "An address that reads state is fetched again whenever that state changes.")
        ])

        Heading("Common mistakes", id: "common-mistakes").h2

        Markdown("**A `match` with no `error` arm.** When the request fails, no arm draws, and the reader is not told:")

        CodeBlock(#"page P(path: "/", title: "Users", description: "Everyone.") {
    resource users = http.get("/api/users")
    Heading("Users").h1
    match users {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .loaded(list) { Text("{list.length} users") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf · draws T21")

        Markdown("Add `error(e) \{ Alert(e.message).danger \}`, or an `else` ([T21](/errors/T21)).\n\n**A field the declared type does not have.** Once a resource is typed, every field read from it is checked against the `type`:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }

page P(path: "/", title: "Users", description: "Everyone.") {
    resource users: [User] = http.get("/api/users")
    Heading("Users").h1
    match users {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .failed(e) { Alert(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list) { for u in list by u.id { Text(u.fullName) } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf · draws T05")

        Markdown("Use the field the type declares, `u.name`, or add `fullName` to the `type` if the server sends it ([T05](/errors/T05)).\n\n**Reading `.data` as if it had arrived.** Before the first answer, `name.data` is `null`:")

        CodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }

page P(path: "/", title: "Users", description: "Everyone.") {
    resource users: [User] = http.get("/api/users")
    Heading("Users").h1
    Text("{users.value.length} users")
}"#, title: "wf · draws T04")

        Markdown("Read it through `?.` with a fallback, `\{users.value?.length ?? 0\}`, or show it inside the `.loaded` arm, where the binding is never null ([T04](/errors/T04)).\n\nGoing further: [Open a socket](/recipes/open-a-socket) keeps a connection open for data the server pushes.")
    }
}
